     1	//! Port of `net.minecraft.world.entity.ai.memory` (26.2 decompile at
     2	//! `/home/eshanki/pumpkin-vanilla-26.2/decompiled`).
     3	//!
     4	//! Stage 0 of the Brain/Memory/Activity port. This module is the *always-live* half of a
     5	//! `Brain`: it is guarded by a short-lived `std::sync::Mutex` and is never taken out of its
     6	//! mutex for the duration of a tick, because memories are legitimately written from outside
     7	//! the owning mob's AI tick (damage handlers, game-event listeners, item pickup). See
     8	//! `super::Brain` for the split and `BRAIN_DESIGN.md` section 4.2 for the derivation.
     9	//!
    10	//! Deviations from vanilla, all deliberate:
    11	//! - `GlobalPos` (dimension + `BlockPos`) is stored as a bare `BlockPos`. Pumpkin has no
    12	//!   `GlobalPos`; the only consumer in this stage is Allay's liked note block, which vanilla
    13	//!   also range-checks per dimension (`AllayAi.java:131`). Cross-dimension correctness is
    14	//!   therefore NOT covered.
    15	//! - `MemoryModuleType.PATH` is not represented at all. Vanilla's `MoveToTargetSink` stores a
    16	//!   live `Path` in it (`MoveToTargetSink.java:92,101`); Pumpkin's `Navigator` owns its path
    17	//!   internally and exposes no equivalent handle, so the slot would be unreadable.
    18	//! - Entity-valued memories hold `Weak<dyn EntityBase>` rather than a strong `Arc`, so a
    19	//!   stale memory cannot keep a despawned entity alive. A failed `upgrade()` is treated the
    20	//!   same as vanilla's "the sensor stopped re-populating this memory".
